#### The same problem at the other end of the scale

The second table shows the opposite end of the range, and the picture barely improves. Only two of the fifteen highest rates are reliable, and eight report a rate of exactly 100% with margins of error between 117 and 236 percentage points, on a scale that can only run from 0 to 100. The problem is not the size of the rate. It is the size of the population underneath it: a tract with a dozen white residents produces an unusable estimate whether the rate comes out at 2% or 100%. 



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
pov_2010 %>%
   filter(!is.na(wht_pct), wht_pct > 0) %>%
  arrange(desc(wht_pct)) %>%
  slice_head(n = 15) %>%
  mutate(
    dot = case_when(
      wht_cv < 12 ~ "🟢",
      wht_cv <= 40 ~ "🟡",
      TRUE ~ "🔴"
    ),
    NAME = str_remove(NAME, ", Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania")
  ) %>%
  select(dot, NAME, wht_pct, wht_moe, wht_cv, wht_reliability) %>%
  gt() %>%
  fmt_number(columns = c(wht_pct, wht_moe, wht_cv), decimals = 1) %>%
  cols_label(
    dot = "",
    NAME = "Tract",
    wht_pct = "Rate (%)",
    wht_moe = "MOE",
    wht_cv = "CV (%)",
    wht_reliability = "Reliability"
  ) %>%
  cols_align(align = "center", columns = dot) %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "Even the highest white poverty rates are mostly unreliable",
    subtitle = "Fifteen tracts with the highest white poverty rates, 2006-2010 ACS"
  ) %>%
  tab_source_note(
    "Reliability thresholds follow Jurjevich et al. (2018): CV < 12% reliable, 12-40% somewhat reliable, > 40% unreliable."
  )
```

